# Problem Statement

## The problem

Most small and mid-sized hospitals and clinics in India still run outpatient
appointments through a paper register or a shared spreadsheet at the front
desk. That arrangement fails in four predictable ways:

1. **Double booking.** Two receptionists, or a receptionist and a phone call,
   promise the same 10:30 slot to two different patients. Nothing in a paper
   register prevents it, and the clash is only discovered in the waiting room.
2. **Stale availability.** A cancellation is noted in the margin, if at all.
   The freed slot is never offered to the next patient, so the doctor sits
   idle while people are turned away.
3. **No record trail.** There is no reliable answer to "what was prescribed
   last visit?", "how many consultations did Dr. Rao complete in March?", or
   "which specialization is over-subscribed?" without manual counting.
4. **No accountability.** Nobody can say who cancelled a booking, when, or
   whether the late-cancellation charge was applied.

The common thread is that a *slot* — a doctor's time between 10:30 and 11:00
on a particular date — is a shared resource being allocated with no mechanism
to guarantee it is allocated exactly once.

## Proposed solution

A role-based console application that treats the slot as the central
resource and makes single-allocation a hard guarantee of the system rather
than a matter of front-desk discipline.

Each doctor is defined by a working window and a consultation length; the
system derives the slot grid for any date from those two values. A booking
request must pass five checks — the time must be a real slot on the grid, it
must be in the future, the doctor must be free, the patient must not be
committed elsewhere at that moment, and the patient must be within the
fair-use cap — and all five run inside a lock held on that doctor. The
concurrency test in the repository starts 25 threads on the same slot at the
same instant and asserts that exactly one succeeds.

Around that core sit user management with hashed credentials and role-based
menus, a prescription record attached to completed consultations, and an
analytics layer that answers the reporting questions from stored data rather
than from manual counting. Every state change is written to an append-only
log file.

## Scope

**In scope**

- Registration and authentication for three roles: Administrator, Doctor, Patient
- Doctor onboarding with specialization, fee, room, working hours, slot length
- Slot-grid generation, availability display, next-free-date suggestion
- Conflict-free booking, cancellation with a late-cancellation charge rule,
  rescheduling with automatic rollback on failure
- Prescription issue and retrieval against completed consultations
- Analytics: status breakdown, revenue by doctor, demand by specialization,
  daily load, top doctors, cancellation rate
- CSV export of the appointment book
- Background reminder and auto-expiry workers
- File-based persistence with an optional JDBC backend behind the same interface
- An automated validation suite covering business rules and the concurrency
  guarantee

**Out of scope** (deliberate, so the in-scope work is done properly)

- Graphical or web interface — the project is a console application
- Real payment gateway integration; billing is computed, not collected
- SMS or email delivery; reminders are written to the log file, with the
  notification hook left as an extension point
- Insurance claims, pharmacy stock, lab orders, in-patient/ward management
- Multi-hospital or multi-branch deployment
- Network access; the application is single-node

## Target users

| User | What they need from the system |
|---|---|
| **Patient** (outpatient, any age group) | See which doctor is free and when, book without a phone call or a queue, cancel or move an appointment, look back at what was prescribed |
| **Doctor** | A clean view of the day's schedule with patient names and stated reasons, one action to close a consultation, a place to record diagnosis and medicines, a summary of work done |
| **Hospital administrator / front desk** | Onboard and deactivate doctors, see the whole day at a glance, answer load and revenue questions from data, export for accounts, audit who did what |

The secondary audience is the course evaluator, for whom the code is written
to be readable: each syllabus concept appears where it is genuinely needed
rather than bolted on, and the README maps units to the classes that use them.

## High-level features

1. **User management** — self-registration for patients, admin-driven
   onboarding for doctors, salted SHA-256 credentials, login attempt limiting,
   account enable/disable, password change.

2. **Slot and booking engine** — grid derived from each doctor's working
   window, FREE/BOOKED/ELAPSED display, five-rule booking validation under a
   per-doctor lock, next-free-date suggestion, cancellation with a two-hour
   late-cancel rule, rescheduling that rolls back if the new slot cannot be
   taken.

3. **Records and analytics** — prescriptions with diagnosis, medicine list and
   advice; revenue, load, demand and cancellation-rate reports built over the
   Collections framework; CSV export; an append-only audit log.

4. **Background processing** — a reminder daemon (extends `Thread`) for
   appointments due within 24 hours and an auto-expiry worker (implements
   `Runnable`) that closes slots which elapsed while still booked.

5. **Pluggable storage** — every service depends on `Repository<T>`, with a
   file implementation used by default and a JDBC implementation configured
   externally, so neither the services nor the UI change when the backend does.
